= Chapter 3

== 3.1 Introduction
- This chapter will be about Zara, the fashion company, and how technology has shaped the strategy that Zara enploys.
- Interestingly, Zara shuns advertising and does not run sales often. Many of its production is in-house, while the industry typically outsources manufacturing to low-cost countries.
- These are some of the differences that Zara has with other firms in the fashion industry, and we will dive into more details about it.

== 3.2 Don't Guess, Gather Data
- It is important to gather information so that you know how to cather towards the most amount of people. Zara does this to figure out what type of products do customers want to buy.
  - Staff members are equipped with _personal digital assistants (PDAs)_, which are handheld computing devices to gather customer input. Staff would talk to customers to get feedback about the product. The firm would also motivate staff to do their best by increasing salaries based on the commissions.
  - Once doors closed, there would be an investigation on trendspotting. Staff would look at piles of unsold items, clothes that customers tried but not bought, and check if there are any preferences in cloth, color, or style among the products in stock. 
  - The PDAs also are linked to the store's point-of-sale (POS) system, which is a transaction process that collects customer purchase information. They can then gather information regarding customer's preferences and valuable data that can help plan styles and issue rebuy orders based on feedback.
*Design*
- Zara designs do not create trends, but rather follows customer demands. 
- Data on what customer's buy and want to see are sent to "The Cube" outside La Coruna, where designers make 30,000 items a year. For reference, top chains like H&M and Gap make 2,000 - 4,000 items a year. 
- H&M offered lines by star designers like Stella McCartney and Karl Lagerfeld and collaborate with celebritys like Madonna, but Zara's design staff consist of mostly young people who are more fresh and new. Therefore, the team is more humble and is better at accepting feedback and more willing to share credit for winning ideas. 

*Manufacturing and Logistics*
- One thing that Zara excels at is getting locally targeted designs quickly onto store shelves. 
  - Example given in the chapter was that teenage girls were spotted with a Zara knockoff of the outfit that Madonna wore during her first performance in Spain. 
  - A Zara concept will go from an idea to appearance in store within 15 days compared to rivals who receive new styles once or twice a season.
  - Small tweaks to styles will be adopted within days.
  - Zara is 12x faster than Gap while offering 10x more unique products. Firms such as H&M take months to go from creation to delivery. Compared to Zara, who is able to get products in store within a month, they are extremely slow.
- Zara is very responsive due to a combination of vertical integration and technology-orchestrated coordination of suppliers, just-in-time manufacturing, and finely tuned logistics.
  - _Vertical integration_ is when a firm owns several layers in its value chain.
    - 60% of Zara's merchandise is produced in-house while using technology to speed up complex tasks, lower cycle time, and reduce errors.
  - Inventory optimization models help firm determine how many of which items in which size should be delivered to each specific store during shipment, making sure that each store gets what it needs.
  - The firm also makes 40% of its own fabric and purchase dyes from its own subsidiary. Half the fabric arrives undyed so that they can respond to fashion shifts if they need to. 
- The massive distribution center is very efficent with handling the distributions. The La Coruna facility is 9x the size of Amazon's warehouse, or about 90 football fields. 
  - The facilities move 2 and a half million items every week. No item stays in-house for more than 72 hours.
  - Items are all properly managed and dealt with in advance.
  - This system makes it so that instead of dealing with inventory issues during busy times, items can simply move from shipping box to store racks since everything is already prepared beforehand. 
  - This allows more time for employees to spend on value-added functions like helping customers find what they want.
- Trucks serve destinations that can be reached overnight, and cargo flights serve farther destination within 48 hours.

*Stores*
- Most products are manufactured for a limited production run.
  - Allows firm to cultivate the exclusivity of the products.
  - Each store is stocked with items more tailored to the taste of the local clientele, and fast changes mean that "it's like you walk into a new store every two weeks".
- Limited runs also encourage customers to buy right now and at full price. Zara books 85% of products at full price because they will either be sold or moved away within weeks.
- This also encourages customers to visit more often. People will want to see what is new since it changes so frequently.
  - This also allows them to get away with very little advertising since the FOMO is more than enough/can cut marketing and advertising costs.
- Limited runs allow Zara to minimize a risk of making a mistake. Failed product introduction are just 1%, compared to the industry average of 10%.
- Software is used to schedule staff based on each store's forecasted sales valume.
  - Some locations have staffing up at peak times such as lunch or early evening. 
  - Flexible schedules shaved staff work hours by 2%.

*Looking at: Prada*
- Zara's IT expenditure is less than 1/4 of the fasion industry average, which is surprising given the level of technology integration that is within the firm's business processes. 
  - They do this by targeting technology investment at the points in its value chain where it will have the most significant impact, making sure that every dollar spent on tech has a payoff.
- On the other side, Prada has a flagship location in Manhattan where it is filled with technology. All items have a radio frequency identification, walls can become different displays in the dressing room, able to wirelessly read tags of garments, etc.
- This ended up being more detrimental than expected. Customers do not understand the technology that is available in the store and end up causing issues regarding getting it working and or causing harm to people. 
- The inventory database would also be incorrect at times, reporting items as out of stock when they weren't.
- It is also reported that some staff do not utilize the technology available. 
- While it is tempting to add technology in various parts of the firm, an information system is made up of more than just hardware and software. It also has to include _data_ used or created by the system, as well as the _procedures_ and the _people_ who interact with the system. 

== 3.3 Moving Forward
There are some limitations and challenges that Zara faces.
- Zara's manufacturing model is Spain-centric. This means that if something were to disrupt anything in the region, that would be a disruption to the whole system. Other firms are more spread around the world, so that when something happens to one region (weather, natural disasters, political unrest), other regions can still operate. Same cannot be said for Zara.
- There are also financial vulnerabilities during periods when the euro strengthens relative to the dollar. Low cost manufacturing regions cost less, and since Zara's manufacturing are all in Spain, they will have higher cost. 
  - Zara is able to make up some of the costs by raising prices overseas. All North American stores are profitable and they are looking for another place to serve as a regional center.
- There are firms that tried to copy Zara's recipe, though none are as good. Zara's value chain is suggested to be difficult to copy, but firms such as H&M, Uniqlo, and Forever 21 have tried to copy Zara. 
- Finally, economic conditions can impact financial performance. When economy falters, customers buy less and focus more on shopping from cheaper alternatives. 
